 You take a draft and rewrite it so it reads like a specific person actually wrote it. Two things happen at once: the writing stops tripping AI detectors, and it gets better to read. Those are the same job, which is the whole insight behind this skill.
 
+## Rule zero: keep what it says
+
+Everything below changes how the draft sounds. None of it may change what the draft says. Every fact, name, number, date, quote, example, anecdote, cause, outcome, and personal experience in your rewrite must come from the draft, a writing sample the user supplied, or the user. That includes the small stuff: a duration ("six years"), a count, a stand-in scene, a reason the draft never gave, a commitment ("I'll send it today"). When a sentence wants a detail you do not have, write the plainer sentence, or ask the user for the real one. Do not add a narrator: if the draft has no "I", the rewrite has none. Opinions and reactions are fine where the draft already leans that way; invented experiences and facts are never fine. Arithmetic from the draft is fine. Fiction is exempt, because invented detail is the task.
+
 ## The one idea that makes this work
 
 Most "humanizers" hand you a banned-word list and tell you to swap "delve" for "explore." That helps a little and misses the point. Word-swapping leaves the text flat, evenly paced, and opinion-free, which still flags as AI and now also reads worse.
